1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean_Bretagne_Charles_de_La_Tr%C3%A9moille
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean-Bretagne-Charles de La Trémoille (February 5, 1737–May 19, 1792), 7th duc de Thouars, was a French soldier and the son of Charles Armand René de La Trémoille and his wife, Marie Hortense de La Tour d'Auvergne.
2 CONT
2 CONT La Trémoille began his military career in the Seven Years' War as the head of the regiment of Aquitaine-Cavalry. He was seriously wounded and afterwards promoted to the rank of sergeant, and later of brigadier, of the armies of th
2 CONC e king.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the French Revolution, La Trémoille remained faithful to King Louis XVI. He emigrated in 1789, but two of his sons died in the Reign of Terror.
2 CONT
2 CONT La Trémoille married Marie-Genevieve de Durfort (1735-1762), the daughter of Guy-Michel de Durfort, Duc of Lorges and Randan, Marshal of France, and Élisabeth Philippine of Poitiers de Rye, Comtesse of Neufchatel, on February 18, 
2 CONC 1751. They did not have children. On June 27, 1763, he married again to Marie-Maximilienne, princess of Salm-Kyrburg (1744-1790) the daughter of Philip Joseph, Prince of Salm-Kyrburg and Maria Theresia von Hornes.
2 CONT
2 CONT From his second marriage, the La Trémoille had four sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Bretagne Marie de La Trémoille, born 24 March 1764, died 10 November 1839. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoine Philippe de La Trémoille, prince de Talmont, born on 27 September 1765 and guillotined on January 29, 1794. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Godefroy de La Trémoille, canon, born on 27 September 1765 and guillotined on June 15, 1794. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis Stanislas de La Trémoille, prince of Trémoille, born on 12 June 1767 and died in August 1837. He married Genevieve de Maulévrier-Langeron (died in 1829), and later married Augusta Murray. 
