1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heinrich_Prinz_zu_Sayn-Wittgenstein
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heinrich_Prinz_zu_Sayn-Wittgenstein
2 CONT
2 CONT Heinrich Prinz zu Sayn-Wittgenstein-Sayn (14 August 1916 – 21 January 1944) was a German night fighter pilot and flying ace during World War II. At the time of his death, Sayn-Wittgenstein was the highest-scoring night fighter pil
2 CONC ot in the Luftwaffe and still the third highest by the end of World War II, with 83 aerial victories to his credit.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born on 14 August 1916 in Copenhagen, Denmark, Prinz zu Sayn-Wittgenstein-Sayn joined the cavalry of the German Wehrmacht in spring of 1937. He was accepted for flight training and transferred to the emerging Luftwaffe. He initial
2 CONC ly served as an observer and then as a pilot in Kampfgeschwader 1 (KG 1) and Kampfgeschwader 51 (KG 51). He saw action with these units in the Battle of France, Battle of Britain, and Operation Barbarossa, the German invasion of t
2 CONC he Soviet Union, before he transferred to the night fighter force. He claimed his first aerial victory on the night of 6/7 May 1942. By October 1942, he had accumulated 22 aerial victories, for which he was awarded the Knight's Cr
2 CONC oss of the Iron Cross on 7 October 1942. He received the Knight's Cross of the Iron Cross with Oak Leaves on 31 August 1943, for 54 aerial victories.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prinz zu Sayn-Wittgenstein was appointed to command Nachtjagdgeschwader 2 (NJG 2) in January 1944, and was killed in action on the night of 21 January 1944. Posthumously he was awarded the Knight's Cross of the Iron Cross with Oak
2 CONC  Leaves and Swords.
